60,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Looking at the candidates, Option B addresses the high demand for general-purpose rooms and the preference for bookable spaces. However, Option A directly solves the manual burden on technicians George mentioned. Which do we prioritize?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, Option B is tempting for users, but it's a ground-floor conversion. @Mizuki's findings on hazardous materials and the cost of off-site space make me wary of the hidden expenses and delays associated with that choice.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,"@Victoria, exactly. Option A is a necessity for our staff. The current extraction equipment is failing them daily. We cannot let our technicians continue this manual drudgery while we chase ""appeal"" projects that often slip.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I agree that the staff need relief, but Option A involves mechanical and ducted plant, which Benjamin noted has seen a twenty percent price hike. We must ensure the budget can actually cover it.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki, you've hit the nail on the head. Option A is a financial gamble given the market surge. However, Option D would likely require the very audiovisual equipment for which we have no renewal reserve. It's a choice between different types of insolvency.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, if Option D is a financial risk, and Option B is a timeline risk, Option A seems the most urgent despite the cost. It solves a tangible operational failure for our staff.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, I am still concerned about the off-site hiring costs if labs are closed. However, compared to the risks of Option B, Option A addresses a critical operational failure. It seems the most justifiable necessity.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"I am glad we are seeing the necessity of Option A. It is not about ""appeal,"" but about basic operational functionality and the well-being of our staff. We must act now.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I can support Option A if we treat it as an essential infrastructure update rather than a comfort project. That should help keep the delivery on schedule and justify the cost.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is heartening to see such a swift consensus. While the price hike is a nuisance, it is far more palatable than the spectral void of an unfunded AV suite or a ground-floor project that lingers for years. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Toshio,,I agree. Option A addresses the operational failures and staff burdens while avoiding the specific risks associated with the other candidates. I vote for Option A.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. While the off-site costs are a concern, the operational necessity for our staff and the risks associated with the other options make this the most rational choice. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,I am fully committed to this. It is the only choice that respects the hard work of our technicians and ensures our labs are fit for purpose. I vote for Option A.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"I agree. By framing this as a necessary infrastructure upgrade, we mitigate the risk of schedule slippage and provide essential relief to our staff. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
